    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205954": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Foundation Relations",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director of Foundation Relations Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director of Foundation Relations Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director of Foundation Relations Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Vice President's Office, IA Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2507 Position ID: 205954 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Reporting to the Senior Director of Foundation Relations, the Assistant Director of Foundation Relations is responsible for providing continued growth for foundation grant programs to secure complex grants, including four to five figure gifts. The Assistant Director works closely with administrators, program directors, clinicians, and primary investigators from Roger Williams, to raise vital funding for the Universities priorities. Pipeline Development Conduct thorough research to identify and assess new prospective funding sources, maintain up-to-date information on foundations, and prepare foundation pro?les in preparation to present the findings to the appropriate groups across campus. Work within the donor database to record actions, update records, track deadlines, and ensure timely and accurate grant reporting communications. Manage an active portfolio of foundation prospects and maintain foundation interactions, including stewardship. Develop and execute ambitious growth plans to raise significant funding from existing and new institutional and private foundations both regionally and nationally. Research new and existing foundation sources to support funding priorities and new initiatives. Grant Writing Develop a broad and deep understanding of current fundraising priorities across campus, as outlined by the President, Provost, Vice President of Research and Academic and Research Deans. Work with faculty and fundraising colleagues across campus to develop proposal concepts that meet potential funders' and RWU's priorities, mission, and needs. Write, edit, and submit letters of intent and full proposals on behalf of the University, serving as the primary author in a team environment. Create compelling and competitive proposals through engaging in productive interactions with faculty and colleagues. Stewardship Collaborate with Principle Investigators, Finance, and the Office of Research and Sponsored Programs to produce and submit grant reports. Plan foundation visits, which may include senior academic, administrative, and volunteer leaders. Develop acknowledgement materials such as letters, press packages, and testimonials to communicate grant impact personalized to each funder. Additional Functions Collaborate with campus partners on new faculty training related to grants and the Assistant Director's role in finding and securing grants, as well as additional outreach activities, and promoting foundation funding opportunities across campus. Organize foundation visits, which may include senior academic, administrative, and volunteer leaders. Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field. Three to five years of professional experience in grant writing and fundraising or a similar content production environment such as journalism, communications, or publishing. Exceptional research and communication skills, both self-directed and within a team environment. Outstanding writing skills, with the ability to tailor a narrative to varying audiences. Superior creative problem solving and systems thinking to effectively match funding needs to opportunities. Excellent interpersonal skills, including the ability to collaborate with campus partners on content development and represent the organization in conversation with potential funders. The ability to give and receive constructive feedback. Knowledge of best practices in philanthropy, higher education, and non-profits Preferred Qualifications Track record of success in securing grants or proposals of $50k or above. Familiarity with RWU's programs and vision. Experience managing or supporting a prospect portfolio. Proficiency in basic graphic design and layout. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203092": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Counseling Center",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Counseling Center Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Counseling Center Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Counseling Center Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Counseling Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Jul 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2830 Position ID: 203092 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Reporting to the Director of the Counseling Center, the Assistant Director of the Counseling Center is responsible for providing a coordinated and comprehensive system of treatment services aimed at enhancing the academic, emotional, and social development and well-being of students while promoting respect for self, others, and diversity within the University community. In collaboration with the Director and the Associate Director, the Assistant Director ensures the successful delivery of all forms of service for the Counseling Center. In the absence of both the Director and the Associate Director, the Assistant Director maintains the operations of the Counseling Center, including treatment services, the facilitation of Center meetings, and external consultations. Clinical Duties Provide individual and group counseling with students experiencing personal concerns while continuously adhering to ethical and legal standards. Conduct crisis appointments, intakes, post-hospitalization meetings, psychological assessments, and other specialized clinical interventions. Continuously compose and update clinical records in a timely manner. Participate in the rotation of on-call and after-hours crisis management, as assigned by the Director. Consult about students of concern and other matters of importance to the Counseling Center with administrators, faculty, staff, family members of students, and on and off-campus treatment providers. Administrative Duties Abide by and maintain Counseling Center and University policies and procedures. In consultation with the Director and Associate Director, continually monitor, assess, and modify, when needed, operations within the Counseling Center. Facilitate Counseling Center meetings and external consultations in the absence of both the Director and Associate Director. Outreach Duties Develop and implement psychoeducational outreach programs, including those requested by administrators, faculty, staff, or student organizations. Serve as the Counseling Center liaison to one or more university departments. Training Duties Contribute to the training and evaluation of graduate-level trainees. Function as an individual supervisor for trainees that includes consistent supervisory meetings and oversight of trainee record-keeping. Provide on-going feedback to trainees and compose and utilize formalized evaluations for trainees. Provide group supervision to trainees as needed. Additional Functions Coordinate, or assist in, Counseling Center efforts in a particular domain (e.g., social media, sports psychology, substance use, Title IX). This is a full-time 10-month position. Requirements: Master's degree in counseling, psychology, social work, or related field. Must hold an active Rhode Island professional license or obtain a Rhode Island professional license within 1 year of start date. Minimum of 1 year of professional experience working with a young adult population. Minimum of 1 year of experience in the training and supervision of graduate-level trainees. Individual and group treatment skills with additional experience in crisis intervention and case consultation. Knowledge and experience in working with diverse populations of students and university personnel. Ability to work non-traditional hours and represent the Counseling Center on an infrequent basis for campus events, emergency situations, and outreach programs. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree (Ph.D., Psy.D., or Ed.D.) in counseling or clinical psychology. One or more internship(s) and/or fellowship(s) in a higher education counseling center. Minimum of one year professional experience in a counseling center within higher education. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204744": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Diversity Office Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2839 Position ID: 204744 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Looking to start or make a move into a career in higher education? If you enjoy working with college students and care about student engagement, mentoring, intercultural learning, leadership, and student success, this may be the role for you. We welcome new professionals, career changers, and applicants from a range of professional and educational backgrounds. If this work interests you and you meet the required qualifications, we encourage you to apply. The Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement is assigned primarily to the Intercultural Center (IC) and reports to the Director of Institutional Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion. The role coordinates student support, academic coaching, mentoring, student leadership development, intercultural education, student success initiatives, and community-building programs for students. The Assistant Director supports students who engage with the IC, with particular expertise in the experiences of first-generation students and students from varied cultural, linguistic, religious, socioeconomic, and educational backgrounds. The role uses an intersectional approach to strengthen access, belonging, retention, persistence, well-being, leadership, and academic success. The Assistant Director supports prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students and contributes to selected alumni-engagement activities. Responsibilities include academic coaching, mentoring, student organization advising, peer mentoring, resource navigation, intercultural programming and education, assessment, communications, and campus partnerships. The role also supports education and campus partnerships related to intercultural dialogue, bias awareness, inclusive student engagement, and broader Division of Equity and Inclusion priorities focused on a thriving campus community. Student Support, Engagement, and Resource Navigation Provide academic coaching, mentoring, referral, and follow-up for students who seek support through the IC, with attention to the educational, financial, cultural, linguistic, and family circumstances affecting student success. Support student access, retention, persistence, belonging, and success through proactive outreach, resource navigation, academic success planning, and timely connection to campus partners. Partner with advising, tutoring, counseling, Residence Life and Housing, accessibility, health services, student conduct, career education, financial aid, faculty, admissions, and other offices to reduce barriers and coordinate student support. Track student contacts, referrals, follow-up themes, academic support needs, and student success trends for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Support prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students through programs and short-term projects tied to access, persistence, retention, and success, and contribute to selected alumni-engagement activities. Intercultural Center Programs, Services, and Events Develop, maintain, implement, and evaluate IC services, programs, events, workshops, and student engagement initiatives. Coordinate programs grounded in intercultural learning, identity development, leadership, dialogue, belonging, and community building. Support inclusive signature programs, heritage and cultural education, recognition efforts, welcome programs, graduation celebrations, academic enhancement workshops, community meals, dialogue programs, and first-generation student initiatives. Develop and support intercultural dialogue, bias awareness, inclusive practice, and community-building education for students, student leaders, campus partners, and assigned groups. Create measurable program goals, learning outcomes, timelines, logistics plans, assessment tools, and post-program reports. Use student feedback and participation data to recommend improvements to IC programs and services. Student Organizations, Leadership Development, and Campus Partnerships Advise assigned recognized student organizations and student leaders connected to the IC, including organizations focused on culture, religion, first-generation student experiences, community engagement, and intercultural learning. Maintain regular engagement with assigned student organizations and student leaders through meetings with organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, area coordinators, and relevant campus partners. Attend selected evening and weekend events as needed. Provide mentorship, continuity, conflict navigation, and leadership coaching for student organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, peer leaders, and emerging student leaders. Support student leaders with program planning, budget management, university policies, risk management, and organizational sustainability. Assist student groups with funding processes, room reservations, purchasing, event preparation, administrative steps, and campus liaison work. Partner with Residence Life and Housing to support approved residential communities through student-leader development, education, programming, community meetings, and referrals. Residence Life and Housing retains responsibility for eligibility, selection, placement, and housing policy. Division Support, Campus Partnerships, and In-Person and Virtual Yield Events Support the work of the Division of Equity and Inclusion, including assigned projects that advance broader divisional priorities. Attend regular one-on-one supervisory meetings with the Director, Center meetings, Division meetings, and other assigned campus meetings. Serve as an active participant on assigned committees, working groups, and campus partnerships related to student access, persistence, retention, belonging, safety, well-being, and success. Participate in admissions and yield events, new student orientation, welcome and move-in programs, open houses, admitted-student events, pre-college programs, alumni events, and outreach activities on and off campus. Partner with alumni, faculty, staff, students, and community partners on short-term projects that support student access, persistence, retention, and success. Assessment, Data, Communications, and Reporting Collect and report appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information consistent with FERPA, university privacy and data-governance requirements, and principles of data minimization. Sensitive student information will be collected only when necessary, maintained with appropriate access controls, and reported in aggregate or deidentified form when feasible. Maintain accurate records related to student contacts, referrals, programs, trainings, spending, attendance, assessment results, student support trends, and annual reporting. Track referrals, follow-up needs, participation, program outcomes, and student support themes for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Prepare concise reports, summaries, and recommendations based on program outcomes, student feedback, assessment findings, and student support trends. Use appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information to identify barriers, assess effectiveness, document impact, and improve IC programs and services. Assist with social media, website updates, newsletters, marketing materials, and other web-based communications for the IC and the Division. Operations, Budget, Safety, and Administration Help manage daily IC operations, including space use, student-facing resources, supplies, equipment, scheduling, calendars, files, and general office workflow. Assist in the supervision and development of undergraduate and graduate student staff and volunteers. Track and report assigned spending in alignment with university policies and purchasing requirements. Report concerns related to safety, facilities, maintenance, accessibility, technology, and operations. Support a welcoming, organized, accessible, and student-centered IC environment. Participate in required training and ongoing professional development. Requirements: Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree. A minimum of 2 to 3 years relevant experience with academic coaching, mentoring, student support, education, or program development, including experience gained through professional roles, graduate assistantships, internships, student leadership, peer education, residence life and housing, new student orientation, admissions, student activities, resource navigation, or related work. Relevant experience includes, but is not limited to, supporting student access, belonging, retention, persistence, and success across varied cultural, linguistic, religious, socioeconomic, and educational backgrounds, including first-generation and multilingual student experiences, with attention to intersectionality. Strong written and oral communication skills across various audiences. Experience using Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, Excel, and related tools. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, education, counseling, social work, sociology, social justice, ethnic studies, gender and sexuality studies, multicultural education, public health, communications, business, or a related field. Professional experience in a higher education, nonprofit, education, community-based, or student support setting related to intercultural student services, multicultural affairs, student support, student success, access, retention, or first-generation student support. Experience supporting student success or access-focused programs, such as first-generation student programs, recognized student organizations, heritage and cultural programming, student recognition programs, residential-community programming, or pre-college access programs. Experience developing or facilitating programs, workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives related to intercultural learning, identity development, belonging, inclusive practice, study skills, student leadership, or community building. Knowledge in one or more of the following areas: student development theory, racial identity development, intercultural learning, belonging research, intersectionality, trauma-informed practice, and campus policies and practices affecting diverse student populations, including student conduct, bias response, privacy, and campus safety. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess student needs, program participation, learning outcomes, student engagement, or program effectiveness. Experience supervising undergraduate students, graduate students, interns, peer educators, or volunteers. Experience with website maintenance, social media, digital communications, marketing, or outreach. Bilingual or multilingual ability, or experience working with multilingual students and families. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following materials: Resume or curriculum vitae. Cover letter describing relevant experience in three or more of the following areas: academic coaching, mentoring, program development, training and education, student success, student support, leadership development, and assessment. Three professional references. For each reference, include the person's first and last name, professional title, organization, phone number, and email address. This position will remain open until filled. Review of applications will begin on Monday, October 5, 2026, and continue on a rolling basis. Candidates are encouraged to apply as soon as possible. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
