    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204743": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Diversity Office Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2838 Position ID: 204743 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Looking to start or make a move into a career in higher education? If you enjoy working with college students and care about student engagement, mentoring, LGBTQIA+ education, leadership, and student success, this may be the role for you. We welcome new professionals, career changers, and applicants from a range of professional and educational backgrounds. If this work interests you and you meet the required qualifications, we encourage you to apply. The Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement is assigned primarily to the Queer and Trans Resource and Advocacy Center (QTRAC) and reports to the Director of Institutional Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion. The role coordinates student support, academic coaching, mentoring, student leadership development, LGBTQIA+ education, and community-building programs for students. The Assistant Director serves all students including queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, and intersex students, as well as allied students. The role uses an intersectional approach to strengthen access, belonging, retention, persistence, well-being, leadership, and full participation in university life. The Assistant Director supports prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students and contributes to selected alumni-engagement activities. Responsibilities include academic coaching, mentoring, student organization advising, peer mentoring, programming, training, assessment, communications, and campus partnerships. The role provides education, student support, and resource navigation related to gender identity, sexual orientation, chosen-name and pronoun processes, facilities, housing, and other student-facing systems. The Assistant Director works in coordination with the supervisor and the university offices responsible for policy, compliance, housing, facilities, and student records. The role also supports broader Division of Equity and Inclusion priorities focused on a thriving campus community. Student Support, Engagement, and Resource Navigation Provide academic coaching, mentoring, referral, and follow-up for students including queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, intersex, and allied students, with particular attention to first-generation students and the ways intersectionality shapes queer and trans student experiences. Support student access, retention, persistence, belonging, identity development, leadership, and full participation in university life. Assist students with resource navigation through established university processes, referrals, and partnerships with responsible offices related to wellness, family concerns, bias experiences, academic success, and campus participation. Partner with advising, tutoring, counseling, Residence Life and Housing, accessibility, health services, Title IX, student conduct, public safety, career education, financial aid, faculty, admissions, and other offices to reduce barriers and coordinate student support. Track student contacts, referrals, support themes, climate concerns, and student success trends for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Support prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students through programs and short-term projects tied to access, persistence, retention, and success, and contribute to selected alumni-engagement activities. QTRAC Programs, Services, and Events Develop, maintain, implement, and evaluate QTRAC services, programs, events, workshops, and student engagement initiatives. Coordinate LGBTQIA+ programs grounded in queer and trans identity development, wellness, leadership, belonging, community building, and joy. Coordinate signature programs and visibility events such as Lavender Graduation, Coming Out Day, Trans Week of Remembrance, Trans Day of Visibility, Pride Week, queer and trans wellness programs, and community-building events. Create measurable program goals, learning outcomes, timelines, logistics plans, assessment tools, and post-program reports. Use student feedback and participation data to recommend improvements to QTRAC programs and services. Training, Education, and Campus Partnerships Develop and deliver education on gender identity, sexual orientation, allyship, pronouns, chosen names, facilities, queer and trans student support, and student-facing systems using approved university information and practices. Provide education and resource guidance to campus partners, in collaboration with the supervisor and responsible university offices, to support queer and trans student engagement and success. Refer questions involving policy interpretation, compliance, student records, housing, facilities, or institutional procedures to the responsible university office. Track training registration, attendance, learning outcomes, feedback, curriculum updates, and follow-up needs. Identify and share approved practices related to queer and trans student support with campus partners and Division leadership. Student Organizations, Leadership Development, and Campus Partnerships Advise assigned recognized student organizations and student leaders connected to QTRAC, including LGBTQIA+, queer and trans, gender justice, and allied student organizations (e.g., SAGA, Feminists United, and the Kinship Collaborative). Maintain regular engagement with assigned student organizations and student leaders through meetings with organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, area coordinators, and relevant campus partners. Attend selected evening and weekend events as needed. Provide mentorship, continuity, conflict navigation, and leadership coaching for student organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, peer leaders, and emerging student leaders. Support student leaders with program planning, budget management, university policies, risk management, and organizational sustainability. Assist student groups with funding processes, room reservations, purchasing, event preparation, administrative steps, and campus liaison work. Partner with Residence Life and Housing to support approved residential communities through student-leader development, education, programming, community meetings, and referrals. Residence Life and Housing retains responsibility for eligibility, selection, placement, and housing policy. Division Support, Campus Partnerships, and In-Person and Virtual Yield Events Support the work of the Division of Equity and Inclusion, including assigned projects that advance broader divisional priorities. Attend regular one-on-one supervisory meetings with the Director, Center meetings, Division meetings, and other assigned campus meetings. Serve as an active participant on assigned committees, working groups, and campus partnerships related to queer and trans student access, persistence, retention, belonging, safety, well-being, and success. Participate in admissions and yield events, new student orientation, welcome and move-in programs, open houses, admitted-student events, pre-college programs, alumni events, and outreach activities on and off campus. Partner with alumni, faculty, staff, students, and community partners on short-term projects that support queer and trans student access, persistence, retention, and success. Assessment, Data, Communications, and Reporting Collect and report appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information consistent with FERPA, university privacy and data-governance requirements, and principles of data minimization. Sensitive student information will be collected only when necessary, maintained with appropriate access controls, and reported in aggregate or deidentified form when feasible. Maintain accurate records related to student contacts, referrals, programs, trainings, spending, attendance, assessment results, student support trends, and annual reporting. Track referrals, follow-up needs, participation, program outcomes, training outcomes, and student support themes for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Prepare concise reports, summaries, and recommendations based on program outcomes, student feedback, assessment findings, and student support trends. Use appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information to identify barriers, assess effectiveness, document impact, and improve QTRAC programs and services. Assist with social media, website updates, newsletters, marketing materials, and other web-based communications for QTRAC and the Division. Operations, Budget, Safety, and Administration Help manage daily QTRAC operations, including space use, student-facing resources, supplies, equipment, scheduling, calendars, files, and general office workflow. Assist in the supervision and development of undergraduate and graduate student staff and volunteers. Track and report assigned spending in alignment with university policies and purchasing requirements. Report concerns related to safety, facilities, maintenance, accessibility, technology, and operations. Support a welcoming, organized, accessible, and student-centered QTRAC environment. Participate in required training and ongoing professional development. Requirements: Bachelor's degree. A minimum of 2 to 3 years relevant experience with academic coaching, mentoring, student support, education, or program development, including experience gained through professional roles, graduate assistantships, internships, student leadership, peer education, residence life and housing, new student orientation, admissions, student activities, resource navigation, or related work. Relevant experience includes, but is not limited to, supporting access, belonging, retention, persistence, and success for queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, intersex, and allied students, including first-generation students and students whose experiences are shaped by intersectionality. Experience developing, implementing, or facilitating programs, events, workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives related to gender identity, sexual orientation, allyship, student support, inclusive practice, social justice education, or queer and trans student engagement. Strong written and oral communication skills across various audiences. Experience using Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, Excel, and related tools. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, education, counseling, social work, sociology, social justice, ethnic studies, gender and sexuality studies, multicultural education, public health, communications, business, or a related field. Professional experience in a higher education, nonprofit, education, community-based, or student support setting related to LGBTQIA+ student services, queer and trans student support, gender and sexuality programs, student engagement, inclusive practice, or queer and trans community building. Experience supporting queer and trans student initiatives, such as Safe Zone education, Lavender Graduation, queer and trans peer mentoring, trans student support, referrals and partnerships with responsible offices, resource navigation through established university processes, or LGBTQIA+ student organizations. Experience designing, facilitating, and assessing recurring education or training programs for students, faculty, staff, or campus partners. Knowledge in one or more of the following areas: student development theory, queer and trans identity development, sexual orientation and gender identity development, intersectionality, trauma-informed practice, and campus policies and practices affecting LGBTQIA+ students, including Title IX, student conduct, bias response, privacy, and campus safety. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess student needs, program participation, learning outcomes, student engagement, or program effectiveness. Experience supervising undergraduate students, graduate students, interns, peer educators, or volunteers. Experience with website maintenance, social media, digital communications, marketing, or outreach. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following materials: Resume or curriculum vitae. Cover letter describing relevant experience in three or more of the following areas: academic coaching, mentoring, program development, training and education, student success, student support, leadership development, and assessment. Three professional references. For each reference, include the person's first and last name, professional title, organization, phone number, and email address. This position will remain open until filled. Review of applications will begin on Monday, October 5, 2026, and continue on a rolling basis. Candidates are encouraged to apply as soon as possible. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205084": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Annual Giving and Leadership Gifts",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Associate Director, Annual Giving and Leadership Gifts Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Associate Director, Annual Giving and Leadership Gifts Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Director, Annual Giving and Leadership Gifts Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Vice President's Office, IA Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2841 Position ID: 205084 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Reporting to the Senior Director of Advancement, the Associate Director of Annual Giving & Leadership Gifts is responsible for coordinating annual giving initiatives, as required.  The Associate Director manages a portfolio of donors and prospects, with responsibility for qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship. The Associate Director of Annual Giving & Leadership Gifts is a hybrid role, combining annual giving program execution with frontline fundraising, strengthening the pipeline of donors who can progress from annual support to leadership and major gift relationships. Annual Giving Program Execution Lead day-to-day implementation of the University's annual giving solicitation calendar in partnership with the Senior Director of Advancement, recommending strategies to strengthen participation, retention, renewal, and revenue. Manage direct mail projects from concept through completion, including coordination with the mail house/direct mail vendor, data requests, segmentation, production schedules, proofs, and delivery. Lead execution of digital annual giving solicitations, including email appeals and other digital outreach, using performance data to refine segmentation, messaging, and strategy. Support annual giving campaigns targeting alumni, parents and families, friends, faculty/staff, and other University constituencies. Help ensure consistent messaging and branding across annual giving communications. Track campaign results and provide reporting on response rates, participation, dollars raised, donor acquisition, retention, and other key annual giving metrics. Collaborate with campus partners. Donor Portfolio Management Manage a portfolio of annual and leadership giving donors and prospects with potential for increased philanthropic engagement. Develop and independently execute individual strategies for qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship, with clear moves-management goals. Conduct meaningful donor outreaches. Personally solicit annual and leadership-level gifts in support of University priorities and maintain measurable portfolio fundraising goals. Identify annual donors with increased capacity or inclination and help move qualified prospects into the University's major gift pipeline. Maintain accurate and timely contact reports, moves, proposals, and prospect information in the advancement database. Partner with frontline fundraisers to facilitate appropriate donor transitions and handoffs. \"Giving Day\" & Special Campaigns Serve as a key operational partner for the University's annual Giving Day campaign. Coordinate campaign timelines, communications, donor segments, challenges, volunteer outreach, and follow-up. Assist with digital and peer-to-peer fundraising strategies. Support targeted annual giving campaigns and special initiatives throughout the year. Evaluate campaign performance and recommend opportunities for improvement. Requirements: Bachelor's degree. Minimum six years of progressively responsible experience in fundraising, annual giving, leadership giving, alumni or constituent engagement, nonprofit development, or a related field, with direct fundraising experience strongly preferred. Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to communicate confidently with donors and prospects. Demonstrated ability to independently manage multiple fundraising projects, donor strategies, vendors, and deadlines simultaneously. Demonstrated experience building donor or constituent relationships, including qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work collaboratively within a goal-oriented advancement team. Willingness to participate in occasional evening and weekend events and limited travel. Preferred Qualifications Four or more years of higher education advancement or nonprofit fundraising experience. Demonstrated experience with direct mail, digital fundraising, and other multi-channel annual giving campaigns. Experience working with donors, alumni, volunteers, customers, clients, or other external constituents. Familiarity with advancement databases, CRMs, or other relationship-management systems. Experience coordinating projects with external vendors. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202317": {
      "title": "Associate Provost for Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Associate Provost for Academic Affairs Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Associate Provost for Academic Affairs Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Provost for Academic Affairs Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Provost's Office Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Jul 8, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2403 Position ID: 202317 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: The Associate Provost for Academic Affairs will join RWU at a pivotal moment: strengthening academic quality, advancing innovation, supporting faculty excellence, and helping shape an integrated undergraduate, graduate, and professional education ecosystem. The Associate Provost for Academic Affairs will provide leadership and administrative support for programs and learning services across academic divisions. The Associate Provost will support all areas of Academic Affairs, with a particular emphasis on faculty development and provide leadership for pedagogical development of full-time and adjunct faculty. This includes the support for faculty learning communities, new faculty orientation, and faculty leadership training. Working with the Provost and Deans, the Associate Provost will support new program development, including graduate programs. Working with the Office of Research and Sponsored Programs, the Associate Provost will guide initiatives that support and promote faculty and student research. The Associate Provost will also work collaboratively with the offices of the Registrar, Student Life, Admissions, Bursar, Institutional Research, Study Abroad, and other university entities and support all institutional accreditation activities in close partnership with the university's accreditation liaison. The Associate Provost provides coordination for academic oversight of international partnerships, including the American University of Greece/ULE. In this newly established partnership, RWU will serve as the degree granting institution for the AUG/ULE. The Associate Provost will work closely with the staff in Athens and in Bristol to ensure the smooth operation and appropriate academic oversight of all programs within the AUG/ULE. The Position Profile may be viewed here . Executive Leadership and Strategic Operations Serve as a senior advisor to the Provost and Senior Vice President for Academic Affairs, advancing institutional priorities and supporting strategic academic initiatives. Act as a primary liaison between the Provost's Office and internal and external stakeholders, fostering collaborative relationships across the university community. Represent the Provost's Office on University committees, task forces, and institutional initiatives as assigned. Lead and coordinate special projects and cross-functional initiatives that advance the mission and strategic goals of Academic Affairs. Contribute to the development, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of institutional policies, procedures, and operational practices. Ensure the highest standards of professionalism, discretion, confidentiality, and ethical conduct in all areas of responsibility. Academic Administration and Institutional Effectiveness Provide leadership in the development, implementation, and continuous refinement of academic policies and procedures to enhance operational effectiveness, compliance, and alignment with institutional priorities. Assist the Provost with review of faculty portfolios submitted for promotion, tenure, and sabbaticals Coordinate new faculty orientation and support faculty integration and success. Assist in the planning and execution of the Fall Faculty Conference and other faculty development initiatives. International Partnerships Serve on the Educational Partnership Committee of the American University of Greece/ULE. Coordinate with the registrars of the ULE and RWU to support academic policy and operations of the ULE. Support initial and periodic academic review by RWU faculty. Serve as a liaison to the EPC Director of the ULE. Work with Study Abroad on the development and maintenance of other international partnerships, including student exchange agreements and faculty led study abroad. Accreditation, Compliance, and Governance Lead and coordinate institutional accreditation activities, with primary responsibility for compliance with NECHE standards and reporting requirements. Partner with the University's accreditation liaison to ensure the timely preparation and submission of all accreditation reports and related documentation. Serve as Institutional Officer for the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC), Human Subjects Review Board (HSRB), and designated grant application processes. Ensure compliance with university policies, faculty contractual obligations, Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) standards, and applicable regulatory requirements governing academic affairs and employment actions. Observe and administer the conditions of the RWU Faculty Association contract. Budgetary and Resource Management Develop, administer, and monitor assigned Academic Affairs budgets with responsibility for forecasting, fiscal planning, expenditure oversight, and resource allocation. Provide strategic fiscal guidance to ensure efficient utilization of resources in support of institutional and divisional priorities. Ensure compliance with university financial policies, procedures, and expenditure controls. Faculty Affairs and Research Support Partner with the Research Office to expand faculty development opportunities that strengthen research, scholarship, and creative activity across the University. Develop and support initiatives that promote faculty and student scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and external funding opportunities. Serve as a liaison to the faculty union (RWUFA), providing guidance and support related to grievance resolution and other faculty affairs matters as appropriate. Staff Leadership and Organizational Development Provide strategic leadership, supervision, and operational oversight for the Honors Program and the Study Abroad Program. Foster a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, and continuous improvement within Academic Affairs operations. International and Academic Partnership Oversight Serve as the University's primary academic liaison to the American College of Greece on matters related to oversight of the University Legal Entity (ULE). Coordinate and verify curriculum assessment processes and approval of new academic programs within the ULE. Ensure appropriate University faculty governance and oversight in the review and approval of curriculum and academic standards. Serve as a member of the Educational Policy Committee of the ULE. Collaborate with the University Registrar to ensure the integrity and effective administration of transcripts, records management, and degree conferral processes related to the ULE. Teaching and Additional Responsibilities Teach one course annually within the faculty member's academic discipline, as assigned. Perform additional duties and responsibilities as assigned by the provost or designee in support of the goals and mission of Academic Affairs. Additional Functions Periodically teach an in-discipline course, schedule permitting and in consultation with the provost. Requirements: Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree and teaching experience in post-secondary education. Five years of experience in a role commensurate with the rank of Associate/Full Professor. Demonstrated, knowledge of the theories of learning and curricular design, and innovative teaching approaches (e.g., collaborative learning, service learning, problem-based learning, writing to learn). Demonstrated knowledge of learning and program assessment strategies. Strong awareness of the needs of students, faculty, the university, and the community. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated record of fostering collaboration and lasting partnerships. Preferred Qualifications Five years of administrative experience at the program, school, or university level. Experience with program development and implementation. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of five (5) professional references, including one each from a supervisor, direct report, and a colleague. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205454": {
      "title": "Director of Intercollegiate Athletics and Recreation",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Director of  Intercollegiate Athletics and Recreation Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Director of  Intercollegiate Athletics and Recreation Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director of  Intercollegiate Athletics and Recreation Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Athletics Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Oct 5, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2791 Position ID: 205454 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Reporting directly to the Vice President for Student Life, the Director serves as the chief administrative and executive officer for RWU's 25 varsity athletics programs, club sports, intramurals, and campus recreation facilities. The Director champions a \"student-athlete first\" NCAA Division III philosophy, cultivating an environment centered on academic achievement, personal well-being, leadership development, competitive excellence, and inclusive community engagement. The next Director will be an approachable, ethical, visible, and collaborative leader who integrates athletics and recreation into the broader fabric of university life. Working in close partnership with institutional leadership, University Advancement, Enrollment Management, and Student Life, the Director will expand philanthropic engagement, champion modern compliance and safety standards, modernize athletic and recreation facilities, and elevate school spirit across the university's coastal Bristol and vibrant downtown Providence campuses. STRATEGIC PRIORITIES FOR THE DIRECTOR - The incoming Director will enter Roger Williams University at an opportune moment of institutional momentum and will be expected to champion three overarching strategic priorities: Navigate the Evolving Division III & NIL Landscape: Establish progressive operational models that uphold rigorous academic integrity, Title IX equity, and athletic competitiveness amid modern NCAA governance and Name, Image, and Likeness developments. Philanthropic Expansion & Facility Modernization: In partnership with University Advancement, execute donor engagement initiatives and capital project strategies to enhance athletic training spaces, student-athlete performance hubs, and recreation facilities. Elevate Campus Spirit & Holistic Student Well-Being: Broaden intramural, club, and fitness engagement across all student demographics, transforming athletic events into unified community gatherings. KEY OPERATIONAL RESPONSIBILITIES Financial Leadership, Advancement & Culture Budget Administration: Direct varsity athletic, club sports, intramural, and recreation budgets within university policy; lead regular reviews with University Finance. Fundraising & Resource Development: Partner closely with Institutional Advancement to execute fundraising strategies that elevate student-athlete success and fund facility modernizations. Athletic Communications & Branding: Elevate the department's profile through contemporary storytelling, social platforms, and brand alignment. Recreation & Wellness Programming: Enhance recreation, intramural, and club sport operations to cultivate student belonging and wellness. Spectator & Game-Day Spirit: Transform home competitions into vibrant, community-wide campus events. Governance, Safety & Compliance Modern Compliance Integrity: Maintain a modern compliance program encompassing NCAA, Title IX, Hazing, eligibility, NIL policies, and monitoring systems that prioritize education, equity, community standards and a safe sport culture. Conference Governance: Actively participate in NCAA, Conference of New England, Interscholastic Sailing Association and other conference affiliations. Recruitment & Enrollment Alignment: Partner with Admissions to attract scholar-athletes dedicated to academic and competitive excellence. Capital Planning & Facilities: Plan, recommend, and coordinate state-of-the-art facility modernizations and maintenance protocols. Risk Management & Safety: Oversee team travel safety, sports medicine coordination, emergency response, and fan event safety. Personnel Supervision: Direct the ethical mentorship, professional development, and formal evaluation of head coaches and administrative personnel. Actively participate in Student Life divisional meetings, initiatives, and strategic planning. Roger Williams University - Director of Intercollegiate Athletics and Recreation Leadership Profile Requirements: Education: Master's Degree in Physical Education, Athletic Administration, Sports Management, or related field required. Administrative Experience: 8-10 years of substantial athletic leadership experience, including at least 5 years in higher education athletic management. Broad Program Competency: Demonstrated expertise in coaching and administrative oversight across varsity, club, and recreation programs. Regulatory Acumen: Strong expertise in Title IX compliance, tort liability procedures, NCAA DIII bylaws, and emerging NIL guidelines. Core Leadership Skills: Strong communication, organizational, leadership, and decision-making skills. Preferred Qualifications Executive Experience: Experience as an Athletics Director or senior Division III athletics administrator. Strategic Management: Strategic planning, facilities management, conference governance, and customer service oriented leadership. Mentorship & Culture: Proven track record of evaluating, mentoring, and developing head coaches and support personnel. Resource Generation: Demonstrated acumen in major gift athletics fundraising and corporate sponsorship cultivation. Safety & Wellness: Comprehensive understanding of sports medicine coordination and student-athlete health protocols. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: The position will remain open until filled; but, for best consideration, candidate applications should be submitted by November 2, 2026 . Inquiries, nominations, and candidate materials will be held in strict confidence. Applicants should submit: A comprehensive letter of interest addressing the responsibilities and qualifications described in this profile. A complete curriculum vitae / resume. Contact details for three (3) professional references (references will not be contacted without prior candidate consent). Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202607": {
      "title": "Director of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center, School of Law",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Director of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center, School of Law Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Director of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center, School of Law Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center, School of Law Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Instruction, SOL Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Jul 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2825 Position ID: 202607 Share About Roger Williams University: Roger Williams University School of Law is a leader in legal education, a go-to source for state and regional legal expertise, and an integral part of legal culture and conversation. As the state's only law school, RWU Law offers a rigorous yet supportive academic environment, a diverse community, the undivided attention of our state bench and bar, exceptional clinical and externship programs, a top faculty, and a broad, accomplished, and growing alumni base. Job Description: Under the direction of the Dean, the Director will lead the programs and initiatives of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center (\"the Center\") at Roger Williams University School of Law. The Center is a sustained, multi-semester advocacy program that integrates coursework, controlled litigation simulations, selective trial competition participation, and legal practitioner engagement. The Center seeks to create world-class trial advocacy practitioners, with nationally recognized excellence. The Director will oversee the Center and its programs and staff and ensure that all Center goals are achieved. In particular, the Director will oversee the Center's day-to-day operations, curricular development and implementation, external engagement, trial team coaches, and program assessment. The Director also will ensure methodological consistency in trial advocacy instruction, following the Mandell Case Framing Method. Program Establishment and Operation In collaboration with the Dean and an advisory board for the Center, the Director of the Mandell Trial Advocacy Center will develop and manage the administrative and programmatic infrastructure of the Center, using the Mandell Case Framing Method as central and essential guidance. Responsibilities will include the following: Develop and manage the administrative and programmatic infrastructure of the Center, using the Mandell Case Framing Method as central and essential guidance. Build and manage a multi-semester trial advocacy curriculum following the Mandell Case Framing Method. Develop and manage a trial advocacy certificate program for law students and practitioners following the Mandell Case Framing Method. Develop and maintain instructional materials for students and practicing attorneys following the Mandell Case Framing Method. Oversee the work of volunteer and adjunct coaches for student trial advocacy teams within the Center. Develop and oversee an annual event in consultation with the Dean. Manage day-to-day operations of all elements of the Center's programming. Support student trial advocacy teams and student participation and travel to trial team competitions. Administer Center funds effectively, in consultation with the Dean, to ensure student access to Center programs and trial team competitions. Support visiting practitioners and national programming engagement. Staff Supervision Hire, train, manage, motivate, and evaluate direct report staff. Requirements: JD from an American Bar Association-approved law school. At least five years of total experience in US civil trial litigation at the state or federal level. Experience in legal education is preferred. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain new programing. Strong organizational, administrative, and communication skills. Ability to work independently with little or no supervision. Proficiency in Microsoft Office. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Salaries for new hires at Roger Williams University usually fall between the minimum and midpoint of the salary range. The salary range for this position is $150,000 - $200,000. Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205065": {
      "title": "Director, Prospect Management (Hybrid)",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Director, Prospect Management (Hybrid) Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Director, Prospect Management (Hybrid) Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director, Prospect Management (Hybrid) Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Development Operations & Donor Services Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2842 Position ID: 205065 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Reporting to the AVP of Institutional Advancement, the Director of Prospect Management is responsible for managing the prospect research program. The Director of Prospect Management, in support of the President's prospect research and prospect management activities, provides crucial assistance to the President and the Institutional Advancement team by establishing and enforcing a \"moves management\" system, based upon comprehensive research that drives mutually-agreed upon cultivation and solicitation strategies for donors and prospects. Prospect Research and Analysis Identify, evaluate and recommend current and potential major gift prospects and assess their ability to financially support the University. Provide research and analysis of biographical, philanthropic, professional and financial information of donors and prospects. Prepare written profiles, rating memos, event bios and qualified lists to support the cultivation of individuals Perform quantitative analysis to determine capacity ratings based on financial data to qualify prospects. Track prospects and donors through monitoring daily news, online \"alerts,\" and utilizing internal information. Track important information on key prospects (such as news and insider stock holdings) and proactively provide this pertinent information to the appropriate relationship manager. Identify donor's associates and acquaintances through relationship mapping to facilitate institutional involvement. Prospect Operations Coordinate all prospect research activities for Advancement. Develop new systems to convert manual processes. Implement the use of AI in the prospect research process to facilitate the efficient development of excellent research products Enhance the understanding of prospect research by Advancement staff through effective communication and training. Represent prospect research best practices at meetings and communicate research techniques and procedures. Implement processes and procedures to enhance prospect research data collection and analysis in the CRM, Raiser's Edge Prospect Strategy Work in partnership with the AVP of Operations for Institutional Advancement to organize and coordinate Pipeline meetings ensuring front line fundraisers have robust, vetted portfolios. Collaborate with front line fundraisers, and other staff during Pipeline meetings to develop cultivation and solicitation strategies and facilitate moves management. Identify new prospects for major gifts initiatives through proactive research and present findings at strategy meetings. Make recommendations regarding potential major donors. Budget Oversight and Reporting Track and manage the prospect research portion of the Advancement budget. Manage and coordinate the evaluation, purchase, budget and renewal of research resources, publications and services. Represent the prospect research arm of Advancement in the evaluation, recommendation and selection of future electronic screening vendors. Research Maintain expertise on research techniques and resources by attending conferences and participating in professional societies. Act as a representative within professional research communities. Salaries for new hires at Roger Williams University usually fall between a minimum and midpoint salary ranges. $71,978 - $78,000 Requirements: Bachelor's degree in related field. Minimum of seven years in a research or research-related environment. Previous development experience and/or a background in a non-profit or educational institution. Ability to communicate with all levels of the organization. Advanced computer hardware and software skills including MS Office Suite, office applications (email, voicemail, etc.). Proficient at scheduling/organizing and presenting data. Proven experience working with spreadsheet, databases, and business applications. Advanced proficiency in Internet research is essential and other research sources. Experience with information resources and publications, including fundraising databases. Ability to supervise and train others. Exceptional communication, writing and editing abilities. Strong research and problem-solving skills. Proficiency in locating and synthesizing material from multiple sources. Ability to interpret, analyze and summarize complex biographical, business and financial data. Preferred Qualifications* Experience with iWave/Kindsight, Lexis-Nexis and other industry resources preferred. Previous campaign experience preferred. Experience with Blackbaud Raiser's Edge preferred * At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
